[{"page":1,"text":"VINEYARD REDEVELOPMENT AGENCY\n\nRESOLUTION NO. U-2010-05 \nA RESOLUTION APPROVING AND ADOPTING THE DRAFT GENEVA URBAN RENEWAL PROJECT AREA BUDGET AS THE OFFICIAL GENEVA URBAN RENEWAL PROJECT AREA BUDGET.\n\nHISTORY\n\nAdopted by Res.\n\nU-2010-05\n on 4/28/2010\n\nVINEYARD REDEVELOPMENT AGENCY\n\nRESOLUTION NO. U-2010-05\n\nA RESOLUTION APPROVING AND ADOPTING THE DRAFT GENEVA URBAN\n\nRENEWAL PROJECT AREA BUDGET AS THE OFFICIAL GENEVA URBAN RENEWAL\n\nPROJECT AREA BUDGET.\n\nWHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of the Utah Community Development and Renewal Agencies\nAct (the “Act”), specifically Utah Code Annotated (“UCA”) § 17C-2-101, the Vineyard\nRedevelopment Agency (the “Agency”) authorized the preparation of the Draft Project Area\nBudget for the Geneva Urban Renewal Project Area (the “Draft Budget” and “Project Area,”\nrespectively); and\n\nWHEREAS, the Draft Budget having been prepared for the Project Area, the Agency held, on April\n28, 2010, a duly noticed public hearing pursuant to UCA § 17C-2-201(2)(d) to allow public\ncomment on the Draft Budget as to whether it should be revised, approved, or rejected; and to\nreceive all written and hear all oral objections to the Draft Budget; and\n\nWHEREAS, having received and heard all commentary on and objections to the Draft Budget\nsubmitted for its consideration, the Agency has passed upon such objections as it has received\nand/or has made such modifications, amendments, and/or emendations to the Draft Budget as it\ndeems appropriate, if any.\n\nNOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED BY THE VINEYARD REDEVELOPMENT\n\nAGENCY AS FOLLOWS:\n\n1. The Draft Budget, a copy of which is attached hereto as Exhibit A, is approved and\nadopted as the Official Budget of the Geneva Urban Renewal Project Area.\n\n2: This Resolution shall take effect upon its adoption.\n\nAPPROVED AND ADOPTED this 28\" day of April, 2010.\n\nBEva RDA Budget","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"EXHIBIT A\n\nGeneva Urban Renewal Project Area Budget\n\nPage 2 of 2; Res. # U-2010-05 - Passing Upon and Overruling Objections to Geneva RDA Budget","ocr":true},{"page":3,"text":"DRAFT PROJECT AREA BUDGET\n\nGENEVA URBAN RENEWAL PROJECT AREA\n\nTHE TOWN OF VINEYARD REDEVELOPMENT AGENCY, UTAH","ocr":true},{"page":4,"text":"TABLE OF CONTENTS\n\nSECTION 1: OVERVIEW & PROJECT DESCRIPTION v.00\n\nDESCRIPTION OF URBAN RENEWAL PROJECT AREA vesssecsetsessesscteens\n\nPROJECT DESCRIPTION wsssesesssesen\n\nSECTION 2: PROJECT AREA BUDGET .ssssrssesessssarseasssneeesnsessuessseonsusruesseussvesiseunessnssustnsanacseysayesenccnnenennnenssuesnennseaneesisesssesest is tasrans\n\nPHASING AND TRIGGER PROVISIONS...\n\nBASE YEAR VALU\n\nPROJECTED TAX INCREMENT REVENUE cssssesesessssnsnnverssoee\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND HOUSING BUDGETS .ccsssssssesssesssunssensetsssssnsssonasersesenensaene setts\n\nPROTECTED TAX INCREMENT REVENUE ~ TOTAL SHARED WITH OTHER ENTITIES\n\nAPPENDIX A: PROJECT AREA Mar...\n\nAPPENDIX B: PROJECT AREA BUDGET... ccssssesssseosussiessevesssnsessssreseecsnasvescssiscanessssctsrstencteensessusesrassannsesacensesasnensssssaseananversarens\n\nAPPENDIX C: PROTECT PHASE ILLUSTRATION asecssssssessssrsssmsavecnaresoeeyassonansesesnserseennsersarssescutebieeccantedtesengoarsesenentronae","ocr":true},{"page":5,"text":"SECTION i: OVERVIEW & PROJECT DESCRIPTION\n\nUnder the Limited Purpose Local Government Entities -Community Development and Renewal Agencies Act, the Town\nof Vineyard Redevelopment Agency (the “Agency”) has the authorization to create an urban renewal project area and form\na Taxing Entity Committee to approve the Agency’s budget for receipt and use of tax increment in order to implement the\n\nGeneva Urban Renewal Project Area Plan,\n\nThe Geneva Urban Renewal Project Area is located on the former Geneva Steel site (which accounts for 1,750 of the total .\n2,055 acres in the Project Area). Geneva Steel closed its operations in 2001, resulting in a loss of thousands of jobs and\nannual tax revenues of approximately $4.5 million. The property taxes based on today’s values are less than $1,000,000\nannually, and the area is blighted with deteriorating and relic structures that will need to be removed and the need for\nenvironmenial remediation. Without public assistance, over 700 acres may be left blighted, and will therefore not make any\nsignificant contribution to the public tax rolls or job creation in the area,\n\n‘The Agency has determined that it is necessary to prepare a Project Area Budget, including a multi-year analysis and\nprojected use of tax increment within the Project Area in accordance with §17C-2-201 & 203.\n\nThe Agency has formed a Taxing Entity Committee (the “TEC”) which is composed of two members from the Utah County\nCommission, two members from Vineyard Town, two members from the Alpine School District (the “District”), one\nmember representing the Staie School Board, and one member representing the remaining taxing entities. The TEC will be\nrequired to review, provide recommendations, and ultimately approve the Budget proposed by ihe Agency.\n\nDESCRIPTION OF URBAN RENEWAL PROJECT AREA\nThe Project Area covers approximately 2,055 acres of land, lies entirely within the boundaries of Vineyard Town and is\n\ngenerally located between Utah Lake on the west and Geneva Road on the east. The majority of the area is owned by a\nsingle owner/developer, Anderson Geneva, LLC, and its affiliates. A map is included in Appendix A.\n\nPROJECT DESCRIPTION\n\nIt is anticipated that over $300 million will need to be investedl in the Project Area in order to clean up contaminaied sites,\nprovide system infrastructure for water, sewer, roads, storm drains, and other basic infrastructure as needed, and to attract\ntop-quality businesses that can provide gaod-paying jobs in the region, The area is projected to develop into an attractive\nmixed-use development composed of office, industrial, retail and residential uses, and will capitalize on mass transit\nopportunities (commuter rail and future light rail), and provide sustainable economic development in the region, At full\ndevelopment, the area will create 5-6 times the number of jobs and result in taxable property values 5-6 times that originally\nprovided by the former Geneva Steel plant.","ocr":true},{"page":6,"text":"SECTION 2: PROJECT AREA BUDGET\n\nThe purpose of the Project Area Budget is to provide the financial framework necessary to implement the Project Area Plan.\nThe following information will detail the sources and uses of tax increment and other necessary details needed for public\nofficials, interested partes, and the public in general to understand the mechanics of the Project Area Budget.\n\nPHASING AND TRIGGER PROVISIONS\n\nThis Budget includes a phasing mechanism. For illustration purposes only, the Budget attached hereto as Appendix B\nillustrates only three phases that are based on the timing of development on hypothetical parcels, rather than on geographic\nlocations within the project area. However, there may be more or fewer phases than three, Each parcel of land within the\nProject Area will be included in one phase only, and will capture increment for a maximum of 25 years. No part of the\nProject Area will capture increment beyond the 40-year tire period shown in the Project Area Budget. The Agency will\ninform the County regarding the triggering of new phases and the property to be included in each phase.\n\nFor purposes of further illustration, a hypothetical “phase” is demonstrated at Appendix C.\n\nBASE YEAR VALUE\n\nState law requires that the base year property tax value for the Project Area Budget will be the total taxable value (including\nreal and personal property) for 2006 which is estimated at $120,131,398 (see Utah Code Annotated §17C-1-102(6)). This\nvalue includes both locally and centrally-assessed properties in the Project Area. However, because individual budgets for\nthis Project Area will be analyzed in appropriate “phases” as the project progresses, the base value for each “phase” will be\nless than the $120 million identified for the entire project area, with the base value for al! phases projected at $120,131,398.\n\nPROJECTED TAX INCREMENT REVENUE\n\nThe Project Area development projections anticipate generating $415 million in tax increment revenues. The Agency\nproposes capturing 75 percent’of the tax increment with a cap of $300 million. If the Agency has not received $300 million\nby Year 40, increment capture will still be discontinued.\n\nTax increment will begin to be generated in 2010 and will reflect the incremental taxable value over and above the base year\nvalue (2006). The Project Area Budget is designed so that the Agency captures 75 percent of all tax increment within the\nProject Area and in each phase for a maximum period of 25 years in each phase and a maximum period of 40 years for all\nphases within the Project Area.\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND HOUSING BUDGETS\n\nThe Agency's administrative allocation under this Budget averages four percent per year over a 40-year period, but ranges\nfrom a high of ten percent in the first budget year to three percent in the last five years. The housing allocation required by\nUCA § 17C-2-303(1)(a) is calculated at 20 percent of tax increment revenues that flow io the Agency each year, and will be\nexpended under the Housing Plan adopted by the Agency.","ocr":true},{"page":7,"text":"PROJECTED TAX INCREMENT REVENUE —- TOTAL SHARED WITH OTHER ENTITIES\n\nAll taxing entities will receive 25 percent of the increment generaiec! from property within the Project Area during each\napplicable 25-year phase (which phases are incorporated! as part of the 40-year period},\n\nj i =NPV.@ 5.0%\n\nUtah County $41,492,261 $3,969,140\n\nCentral Utah Water Conservancy $3,821,201 $1,319,747\n[pine School District $72,039,185 . $24,880,534\nneyard $16,192,338 $5,592,429\n\nNorth Utah Water Conservancy” $229,272 $79,185\n\nS“YOTAL : “ $103,774,256 Soe